WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:05.920
The story of Despicable Me 3 was a much tougher
nut to crack for its screenwriters than the

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:09.840
first two films in the franchise, which means
that there are a number of scenes that wound

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.129
up being deleted and several concepts that
ended up rejected from the final version of

00:00:14.129 --> 00:00:16.190
Illuminations' latest animated movie!

00:00:16.190 --> 00:00:20.330
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here,
and in this video I'm going to reveal and

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:24.260
explain some of the scenes and ideas that
were cut from the theatrical release of Despicable

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:25.260
Me 3.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:28.860
I've also got several sets of awesome merchandise
from the movie to give away.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:33.140
For a chance to win, make sure you subscribe
and turn on your notifications, and answer

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:35.370
the question I'll be asking later in the video!

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:39.270
A quick reminder: there are some spoilers
in this video, so take care if you haven't

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:41.290
seen the movie yet.

00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:45.800
Despicable Me 3 screenwriters Cinco Paul and
Ken Daurio have said they had trouble finding

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:47.800
the right role for Lucy in this movie.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:52.130
Indeed, in early drafts of the script, Lucy's
role was going to be very different to what

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:54.030
we see in the final movie.

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:58.520
Originally, once Gru and his family arrived
in Freedonia, Lucy was going to start fighting

00:00:58.520 --> 00:00:59.550
crime there.

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:03.379
Actually, if you think about it, this storyline
would have fit in rather nicely with how Lucy's

00:01:03.379 --> 00:01:07.570
character was first established in Despicable
Me 2 where she was an undercover agent for

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:11.680
the Anti-Villain League and obviously enjoyed
her job fighting crime, so it makes sense

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:15.429
she would miss that part of her life when
she and Gru are fired from the AVL in this

00:01:15.429 --> 00:01:16.429
movie.

00:01:16.429 --> 00:01:20.759
And given the way Lucy used various gadgets
including a tranquilizer gun and lipstick

00:01:20.759 --> 00:01:24.869
taser in the second film, I can't help but
wonder what new gadgets she would have used

00:01:24.869 --> 00:01:30.149
in her crime-fighting in Despicable Me 3 if
the filmmakers had gone ahead with that storyline.

00:01:30.149 --> 00:01:33.780
What we do know though is that during the
movie's Cheese Festival in Freedonia, there

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:37.820
was a big scene cut which would have featured
Lucy chasing after a robber who'd stolen an

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:39.439
old woman's purse.

00:01:39.439 --> 00:01:43.560
As you can imagine from the kind of humour
we see in the Despicable Me movies, this deleted

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:47.729
scene was full of slapstick moments as well
as cheese-based puns and sight gags.

00:01:47.729 --> 00:01:52.419
For instance, Lucy was going to have to dodge
a whole load of statues made of cheese and

00:01:52.419 --> 00:01:57.670
other cheesey stuff as she raced after the
purse-snatcher, and when she finally caught

00:01:57.670 --> 00:02:01.490
him she was going to tell him that 'This is
nacho day!'

00:02:01.490 --> 00:02:06.119
In the end though, the screenwriters decided
Lucy's vigilante storyline was too disconnected

00:02:06.119 --> 00:02:09.920
from the rest of the movie and they wanted
her story to have more of an emotional pay-off

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:14.740
this time, so they focused their attention
on building up Lucy's relationship with Margot,

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:18.500
Edith, and Agnes, and how she deals with becoming
their step-mum.

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:22.740
In fact, that decision meant that Lucy's storyline
in this film took a similar path to Gru's

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:27.110
in the first Despicable Me movie, as both
Lucy and Gru face challenges becoming a parent

00:02:27.110 --> 00:02:30.830
to the girls, but end up becoming close to
them and later save their lives after they're

00:02:30.830 --> 00:02:33.140
kidnapped by the movie's villain.

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:36.860
Despite all that, I think I still would have
preferred the more active and action-packed

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:39.900
crime-fighting storyline for Lucy in this
movie.

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:45.450
Although former child star Balthazar Bratt
is very clearly the new big bad who Team Gru

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:50.460
has to defeat in Despicable Me 3, the filmmakers
didn't always have Bratt in mind to be their

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:52.030
new movie's number one villain.

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:56.040
You see, one of the first ideas for the movie
was that Bratt was just a henchman to another

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:58.690
big supervillain who was the main focus of
the film.

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:03.011
However, that original supervillain was pretty
humourless, and just didn't work for the movie

00:03:03.011 --> 00:03:08.030
because, as Despicable Me 3 producer Chris
Meledandri has said, 'you need to give Gru

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:11.930
a worthy adversary – someone who presents
a real challenge [….], as well as someone

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:13.150
who is worthy comedically.

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:17.470
Gru is so fun and lively, that if you put
a very straight villain against him, that

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:18.810
character will just disappear.'

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:23.790
Fortunately, when the filmmakers saw the animators'
early sketches of Balthazar Bratt, they realised

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:28.010
that his character had a lot more potential
than they first thought and that he was actually

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:30.690
the 'worthy adversary for Gru' that they'd
been looking for.

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:35.730
So, they upgraded Bratt's role from a mere
side-kick to a 1980s-obsessed supervillain

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:37.190
out to destroy Hollywood.

00:03:37.190 --> 00:03:42.960
Speaking of Balthazar Bratt's fixation with
all things 1980s, of course, that means the

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:47.510
movie contains a ton of pop culture references
to fashion, TV shows, movies, and music from

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:48.510
that decade.

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:52.630
But not everything the screenwriters had in
mind to show off Bratt's 80s mania made it

00:03:52.630 --> 00:03:54.600
into the final cut of the film.

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:58.690
For example, Trey Parker, who provides the
voice of Balthazar Bratt and is best known

00:03:58.690 --> 00:04:03.450
as co-creator and star of South Park, recorded
a bunch of songs for use in the movie including

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:08.560
Karma Chameleon, a classic song from 1983
by the British band Culture Club.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:12.320
However, those songs ended up on the cutting
room floor when the scene where Bratt was

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:15.940
going to sing a track from the 1980s was completely
dropped from the movie.

00:04:15.940 --> 00:04:20.150
However, we do still get to hear voice actor
Trey Parker sing in the movie, but now it's

00:04:20.150 --> 00:04:24.189
a new track called Hug Me which was written
by Pharrell Williams.

00:04:24.189 --> 00:04:28.490
By the way, I've made a separate video about
all the cool easter eggs and movie references

00:04:28.490 --> 00:04:32.729
in Despicable Me 3, and there's a link to
that in the video description below and at

00:04:32.729 --> 00:04:34.840
the end of this video.

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:39.940
Long-lost twin brothers Gru and Dru are opposites
in many ways and their contrasting personalities

00:04:39.940 --> 00:04:42.520
influenced the filmmakers' design of Dru.

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:47.759
In fact, Dru's look in the final film, including
his flowing blond hair and white suit versus

00:04:47.759 --> 00:04:52.520
Gru's bald head and dark clothes, was one
of co-director Eric Guillon's first designs

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:53.520
for Dru.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:58.100
However, before the filmmakers finally decided
on that particular look for Gru's new brother,

00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:03.219
they tried out a variety of other looks, including
having Dru wear a big fur coat over a sweat

00:05:03.219 --> 00:05:08.400
shirt, a design which emphasised that Dru
was both very laid-back and very wealthy thanks

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:12.199
to having inherited his father's enormous
fortune and pig farm.

00:05:12.199 --> 00:05:16.150
In the end though, that look was rejected
in favour of their first design which the

00:05:16.150 --> 00:05:20.479
filmmakers felt reflected Dru's personality
in a much simpler and clearer way.

00:05:20.479 --> 00:05:25.259
The clear-cut differences between the newly-reunited
twin brothers are played up during a family

00:05:25.259 --> 00:05:28.199
dinner scene, which was also the subject of
several cuts.

00:05:28.199 --> 00:05:32.449
This scene where Gru and Dru swap clothes
and hairstyles, and generally clown around

00:05:32.449 --> 00:05:36.319
pretending to be each other, started out quite
a bit longer than the scene we see in the

00:05:36.319 --> 00:05:40.860
final film, with the brothers' back-and-forth
banter initially stretching out to over 3

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:41.860
minutes.

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:46.280
The scene was actually rather complicated
as Steve Carell, who voices Gru and Dru, had

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:50.529
to play each character imitating the other
character in a conversation between themselves!

00:05:50.529 --> 00:05:55.569
So, to make it easier, Carell recorded all
Gru's lines first, then went back and recorded

00:05:55.569 --> 00:05:57.539
all Dru's lines later.

00:05:57.539 --> 00:06:02.029
There's also another funny moment that takes
place at Dru's mansion in Freedonia which

00:06:02.029 --> 00:06:03.850
was cut from the final movie.

00:06:03.850 --> 00:06:08.340
In the deleted scene, Gru and Dru are wearing
their new spy costumes and are examining one

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:12.819
of Dru's many weapons when, suddenly, Lucy
walks in on them and is pretty annoyed as

00:06:12.819 --> 00:06:15.680
she thinks they're now both going to be villains!

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:17.909
"So, you two are villains now?!"

00:06:17.909 --> 00:06:24.189
"Whaaaaaa ha ha ha ... I mean hello sweetie"
"Uh oh, busted!"

00:06:24.189 --> 00:06:28.370
Although this scene ended up being deleted,
it looks like there's still a little trace

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:32.550
of what the filmmakers were going for with
this moment in the final film.

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:37.309
Just after Lucy sends away Niko's mother for
cursing Margo, she walks towards the window

00:06:37.309 --> 00:06:42.539
in Dru's mansion and spots Gru and Dru in
their spy costumes jetting off in their speedboat.

00:06:42.539 --> 00:06:46.830
Lucy looks pretty annoyed, like she knows
something is going on and thinks the brothers

00:06:46.830 --> 00:06:50.719
are up to no-good, which likely relates back
to that deleted scene where she discovered

00:06:50.719 --> 00:06:52.370
Gru and Dru in their lair.

